Boston is a well-balanced team led by Adam Oates' stickhandling and passing and Ray Bourque's checking.  Cam Neely and import Vladimir Ruzicka make major contributions on offense.Buffalo is a rough and tumble team.  The top line of Pat LaFontaine, Alexander Mogilny and Dave Andreychuk provide the offensive punch.  Rob Ray, Brad May and Gord Donnelly can mix it up with any team on ice.  Calgary is led by 50-goal scorer Gary Roberts and Al MacInnis' awesome slap shot.  Craig Berube and Joel Otto like to make their presence felt, particularly on the bodies of opposing forwards.Chicago is the hardest checking team in the game.  Defensemen Chris Chelios, Steve Smith and Keith Brown all know how to deliver a hit.  50-goal scorer Jeremy Roenick plays brilliantly on offense and goalie Ed Belfour is likely to stop any puck that nears the net.Detroit can skate with any team.  Sergei Federov, Steve Yzerman, and Paul Ysebaert are the offensive sparkplugs, and Steve Chiasson and Brad McCrimmon provide strong defensive support.  Bob Probert is always around if the other team decides to get rough.Edmonton is a balanced team led by the scoring prowess of Vincent Damphousse and Joe Murphy, the speed of Petr Klima and the body-flipping checks of Esa Tikkanen.  Bill Ranford and Ron Tugnutt head up the goaltending effort.Hartford sports one of the up and coming teams.  Pat Verbeek and John Cullen provide most of the offense.  Expect the goalies, led by Kay Whitmore, to see lots of action.Long Island has a handful of scorers, namely Pierre Turgeon, Adam Creighton and Derek King, but the defense is often overburdened as many opposing shots find the net.Los Angeles has lots of experienced superstars, like Wayne Gretzky, Paul Coffey, and Jari Kurri, and talented forwards Luc Robitaille and Tomas Sandstrom.Minnesota relies on Mike Modano, Ulf Dahlen, and Dave Gagner for offense and strong goaltending by Jon Casey and Darcy Wakaluk.  Montreal is led by Patrick Roy, the best goaltender in the game.  Superior offensive skills are demonstrated by Denis Savard, Kirk Muller and rookie Gilbert Dionne, while the defense is deep and talented.New Jersey has one of the sharpest shooting teams in the game, led by offensive powerhouses Claude Lemieux, John MacLean and Stephane Richer. Scott Stevens is particularly tough on defense.New York may be the best team in the game, led by superstar Mark Messier and Mike Gartner.   Brian Leetch and James Patrick are sharp on defense, and goalies John Vanbiesbrouck and Mike Richter won't allow many goals.Ottawa is an expansion team in its first season.  Jeff Lazaro and Sylvain Turgeon provide the leadership.  Go for the ultimate challenge --- take this team to the second round of the NHLPA playoffs.Philly's offense relies heavily on contributions from the recently acquired Mark Recchi and Rod Brind'Amour.  Ron Hextall's brilliant goaltending often saves the day.Pittsburgh is a team of stars.  Mario Lemieux, Kevin Stevens, Joe Mullen, Rick Tocchet, and young Jaromir Jagr provide constant action with a variety of signature moves.  An exciting team to play and watch.Quebec is an up and coming team.  Owen Nolan, Mats Sundin, and Joe Sakic have their moments.  Jacques Cloutier and Stephane Fiset are busy in goal.San Jose is one of the most popular teams in the game.  Highlights include Doug Wilson's glass-breaking slap shot, Pat Falloon's clutch goal scoring ability, and Link Gaetz's checking.St. Louis' shooting capability is led by ultimate offensive weapon Brett Hull on shots set up by Craig Janney and Brendan Shanahan.  Curtis Joseph is the big man in goal.Tampa Bay is one of the more contact-oriented teams.  Basil McRae, Tim Hunter, and Mike Hartman lead the action.Toronto's offense is provided by Doug Gilmour and Glenn Anderson.  Superstar Grant Fuhr makes the big plays in goal.Vancouver is a talented team led by speedskaters Pavel Bure, Cliff Ronning and Geoff Courtnall.  Trevor Linden and Igor Larionov provide instant offense.  Kirk McLean is particularly tough in goal.Washington is a solid team led by goalie Don Beaupre, Dino Ciccarelli and Dimitri Khristich.  Al Iafrate and Kevin Hatcher are aggressive checkers on defense.Winnipeg is an up and coming team led by Bob Essensa's strong goaltending and Phil Housley's brilliant all around play.  This team needs more guys like Ed Olczyk and Pat Elyniuk who can stick the puck in the net.All Stars East is led by Patrick Roy in goal, and Mario Lemieux and Mark Messier on offense.  Ray Bourque, Brian Leetch and Paul Coffey are offensive-minded defensemen.  All Stars West is led by Brett Hull, Wayne Gretzky, and Luc Robitaille.  Al MacInnis and Doug Wilson both have awesome glass-breaking slapshots.  Ed Belfour keeps the goal net in brand new shape.Welcome to ~.I'm Ron Barr for EASN.And in goal tonight, [ for { and ] for }.